Complexity

For each next():

Worst-case time: O(n)
Space: O(n)

For n calls:

O(n²)

This is too slow for a large number of prices.

Approach 2 — Stack of Prices

We can use a monotonic decreasing stack.

Instead of repeatedly checking all previous prices, maintain only the prices that can potentially act as a previous greater element.

Idea

For current price:

while stack.top <= price:
    pop

After popping:

If stack is empty → today's span includes all previous days.
Otherwise → stack top is the previous greater price.

But there is a problem.

If we store only prices, we still need to know how many days each popped price represents.

So we need another structure to store spans.

Approach 3 — Stack of (price, span)

Store:

(price, span)

For every new price:

span = 1

Then:

while top.price <= price:
    span += top.span
    pop

Finally:

push(price, span)

This is the key optimization.

Code
class Node {
    int price;
    int span;

    Node(int price, int span) {
        this.price = price;
        this.span = span;
    }
}

class StockSpanner {

    Stack<Node> stack;

    public StockSpanner() {
        stack = new Stack<>();
    }

    public int next(int price) {

        int span = 1;

        while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek().price <= price) {

            Node node = stack.pop();

            span += node.span;
        }

        stack.push(new Node(price, span));

        return span;
    }
}

This is essentially the same idea as your solution.

Approach 4 — Your Solution ⭐

Your implementation is the standard optimal solution.

You use a Node containing:

data  → stock price
count → span represented by that price
Your Code
class Node{
    int data;
    int count;
    
    Node(int data, int count){
        this.data=data;
        this.count=count;
    }
}

class StockSpanner {
    private Stack<Node> stack;
    
    public StockSpanner() {
        stack=new Stack<Node>();
    }
    
    public int next(int price) {
        int count=1;
        
        while(!stack.isEmpty()&&stack.peek().data<=price){
            Node node=stack.pop();
            count=count+node.count;
        }

        stack.push(new Node(price, count));
        
        return count;
    }
}

Why does this work?

Suppose our stack is:

(100,1)
(80,1)
(60,1)
(70,2)

The node:

(70,2)

means:

Price 70 represents 2 consecutive days: 70 and 60.

Therefore, when a new price 75 arrives, instead of individually checking 70 and 60, we can simply do:

75 >= 70

and add:

2

to the span.

This is called span compression.

