Why Is the Stack Monotonic?

Look at the prices in the stack:

100
80
75

They are decreasing from bottom to top.

100 > 80 > 75

Whenever a new price arrives, all smaller/equal prices are removed.

For example, when 85 arrives:

100
80
75

75 is removed because:

75 <= 85

Then 80 is removed:

80 <= 85

But 100 stays:

100 > 85

So 100 becomes the previous greater element.

Why Can We Pop an Entire Span?

This is the most important part of the problem.

Suppose stack contains:

(80,1)
(70,2)

(70,2) represents:

70
60

Now 75 arrives.

Instead of checking:

75 >= 70
75 >= 60

we already know that the span represented by 70 is 2.

So:

count += 2

This gives:

1 + 2 = 3

That's why storing the span along with the price makes the algorithm efficient.

Complexity
Brute Force

For each price, we may scan all previous prices.

Time per next() = O(n)

Over n calls:

O(n²)

Space:

O(n)
Monotonic Stack

Each price is:

pushed exactly once
popped at most once

Therefore, although one particular next() can pop many elements, across all calls each element is popped only once.

Total:

O(n)

Space:

O(n)
Final complexity
Time  = O(n) amortized
Space = O(n)

For the next() operation, we usually say amortized O(1).

Approach Comparison
Approach	Idea	Time	Space
Brute Force	Scan backward every time	O(n²) total	O(n)
Stack of Prices	Monotonic stack	O(n) total*	O(n)
Stack (price, span)	Compress previous spans	O(n) total	O(n)
Your Node Stack	Same (price, span) optimization	O(n) total	O(n)

*A stack containing only prices isn't enough by itself to directly recover spans efficiently unless additional indexing/span information is maintained.

The Core Pattern

This problem is a variation of:

Previous Greater Element

For every current price, we want to find the closest previous price that is:

> current price

Everything between that greater price and today contributes to the span.

The monotonic stack efficiently finds that boundary.

